        <div class="case-visual-abstract">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M4 8V5a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h3M16 4h3a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v3M20 16v3a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-3M8 20H5a1 1 0 0 1-1-1v-3" />
            <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3" /><path d="M7 18c.8-2.4 2.7-3.5 5-3.5s4.2 1.1 5 3.5" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="case-body">
          <span class="case-tag">iOS · On-device ML · Security</span>
          <h3 class="case-title">YEO Messaging: verify the person, not the device</h3>
          <p class="case-problem">
            <strong>Challenge:</strong> A secure messenger needed to confirm that the intended recipient is the person reading a message, not just whoever is holding their unlocked phone.
          </p>
          <p class="case-outcome">
            <strong>Approach:</strong> As Lead iOS Developer, built the recipient-verification pipeline with CoreML and ARKit, running face recognition on the device, alongside OLM end-to-end encryption and WebSocket messaging.
          </p>
          <div class="case-stack">
            <span>CoreML</span><span>ARKit</span><span>OLM</span><span>GraphQL</span><span>WebSockets</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- FRiNGE — real-time livestream platform -->
      <article class="case-card case-card--text case-card--wide">
        <div class="case-visual-abstract">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2.5" /><circle cx="4" cy="6" r="1.75" /><circle cx="20" cy="6" r="1.75" />
            <circle cx="4" cy="18" r="1.75" /><circle cx="20" cy="18" r="1.75" />
            <path d="M6 7.2 10.2 11M17.8 11 22 7.2M6 16.8 10.2 13M17.8 13 22 16.8" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="case-body">
          <span class="case-tag">iOS · Real-time video · Location</span>
          <h3 class="case-title">FRiNGE: location-based livestreaming</h3>
          <p class="case-problem">
            <strong>Challenge:</strong> Going live outdoors means slow, flaky networks and a camera that has to move cleanly between capture and the live RTC session.
          </p>
          <p class="case-outcome">
            <strong>Approach:</strong> As Head of Mobile and the only mobile lead, designed and shipped the iOS app end to end: led the architecture for Go-Live performance and the camera ↔ Agora handoff, added token prefetch and warm-restart flows so going live feels instant, and built on Swift 6 strict concurrency.
          </p>
          <div class="case-stack">
            <span>Swift 6</span><span>SwiftUI</span><span>Agora RTC</span><span>Supabase</span><span>PostHog</span>
          </div>
        </div>
